From 3a792516ba6381c1578b7585e33f8e093608c09f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Mutai <official.daniel.mutai@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 08:58:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(label-ocr): capture in-page instead of handing off to OS
 camera

Symptom: on the installed PWA, tapping the confirm/tick button after
taking a label photo returned to the homepage with no result applied.

Root cause: label capture used <input type="file" capture="environment">,
which hands off to the OS's native camera app and back. Installed PWAs
(especially iOS Safari standalone) frequently get fully reloaded by the
OS on that handoff to free memory, wiping all JS state - including the
in-flight promise waiting on the photo - and dropping the user back at
the app's start_url. This is a structural issue with that capture
method inside a PWA, not something fixable by patching the handler.

Fix: capture the photo in-page instead, the same way BarcodeScannerSheet
already does reliably - getUserMedia for a live camera feed, a shutter
button, and a canvas snapshot on tap. The browser never leaves the app,
so there's no OS handoff and no reload risk.

- useLabelOcrCapture: reworked from 'trigger a hidden file input' to
  'process an already-captured image blob' (processImage()). The OCR +
  AI-parse pipeline itself is unchanged.
- New LabelCaptureSheet: in-page camera capture UI, mirrors
  BarcodeScannerSheet's camera setup/teardown almost exactly. Shows a
  preview + 'reading the label' state while processing, and a retry
  option if nothing useful was extracted.
- TopBar and AddItemSheet both now open LabelCaptureSheet instead of
  triggering a hidden input; the onCapture callback wiring (sessionStorage
  handoff for TopBar, direct form state for AddItemSheet) is unchanged
  from the previous implementation.

Verified with tsc --noEmit on the frontend workspace (0 errors).
